1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,790
Two bright shop windows display the same
percentage increase.

2
00:00:03,790 --> 00:00:08,673
Both price tags rise by ten percent, but
the waiting crowds begin to move

3
00:00:08,673 --> 00:00:09,022
differently.

4
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:18,757
One window starts with many buyers and a
low price.

5
00:00:18,757 --> 00:00:22,795
The other begins with fewer buyers and a
higher price, changing the comparison

6
00:00:22,795 --> 00:00:23,106
base.

7
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:33,663
A single straight demand line connects
both windows.

8
00:00:33,663 --> 00:00:38,559
Its slope stays constant, yet price and
quantity ratios change as the marker

9
00:00:38,559 --> 00:00:39,689
moves along it.

10
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:52,742
A small loss of buyers can be a tiny share
of one crowd and a large share of

11
00:00:52,742 --> 00:00:55,106
another. Elasticity compares these
proportional responses.

12
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:03,885
Price and quantity now form rectangles of
revenue.

13
00:01:03,885 --> 00:01:08,738
One rectangle grows after the increase,
while the other shrinks, despite the

14
00:01:08,738 --> 00:01:10,356
matching percentage price changes.

15
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:18,516
The two windows pause beside the demand
line.

16
00:01:18,516 --> 00:01:23,173
Why does the same ten percent increase
cause a different shock, and which

17
00:01:23,173 --> 00:01:24,606
measure explains the difference?
